use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../Link import Link
from ../../buttons/Button import Button

export ui-component Pagination:
    description:
        Pagination splits a long set of results into pages and lets the user
        move between them. It shows which page they are on, the pages around
        it, and ways to go to the previous and next page.

    composes:
        - @{Link}
        - @{Button}

    counting:
        The component says how many items there are in total and how many go
        on each page, and pagination works out how many pages that makes.
        Pages are numbered from 1.

    pages:
        The first and last pages are always shown, along with a few pages
        either side of the current one. Pages left out are replaced by an
        ellipsis, which can't be pressed. The component decides how many pages
        show either side. With only a few pages, every one is shown.

    current:
        The current page stands out, and assistive tools are told it is the
        current page. Pressing it does nothing.

    previousAndNext:
        Buttons or links go to the previous and next page, and optionally
        straight to the first and last. At the first page, the ways back are
        unavailable, and at the last page, the ways forward are. As a link has
        no disabled state, an unavailable link is shown as disabled plain text
        instead.

    linksOrButtons:
        When each page has its own address, the page items are Links, so the
        user can open a page in a new tab, bookmark it, or share it. Otherwise
        they are Buttons, and pressing one changes the page in place. The
        component decides which.

    names:
        The whole control has a name, such as "Search results pages", so it
        can be told apart from other navigation. Each item has a name that
        reads well on its own, such as "Page 3" or "Next page", even if it
        only shows a number or an arrow.

    pageSize:
        The component can let the user change how many items show on each
        page, usually with a Select next to it. Which page they land on after
        changing it is up to the chosen library, but it is never a page that
        doesn't exist.

    focus:
        Changing the page in place doesn't move focus. Whether to take the
        user back to the top of the results is up to the component.

    nonGoals:
        - Doesn't fetch or slice the results. It only says which page is wanted
        - No endless scrolling or "Load more" button
        - No box for typing a page number. Pair it with a NumberInput if that
            matters
